"use strict";
window.LC_CHUNKS=window.LC_CHUNKS||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]=window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"]||{};window.LC_CHUNKS["university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en"][23]={"university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M23-L1":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L1\">M23-L1 – Generative Probabilistic Models and Sampling<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Generative Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> M18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Distinguish generative modelling\nfrom classification and investigate a mixture distribution analytically\nand through sampling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From observations to a distribution<\/h2>\n<p>Quality control produces measurements originating from two unknown\noperating states. Rather than merely classifying a measurement as normal\nor unusual, we want to describe the distribution of measurements and\nsimulate new plausible values. A model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis therefore fitted to a sample. A conditional model\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(x\\mid c)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngenerates data for a specified context\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nBy contrast, a discriminative model might estimate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>y<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(y\\mid x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nit need not model the distribution of the inputs.<\/p>\n<p>Three goals may diverge: high test likelihood, realistic samples and\nuseful latent features. Some models provide an evaluable density,\nwhereas others only specify a method for generating samples. For\ncontinuous variables, a density is not a point probability. It is\ndefined with respect to a reference measure; only its integral gives a\nprobability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A bimodal measurement distribution<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001649_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Two weighted normal curves centred at minus 2 and plus 2 form a bimodal mixture density.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Two weighted normal curves centred at\nminus 2 and plus 2 form a bimodal mixture density.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Mixture of two operating-state densities.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">{<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">}<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z\\in\\{0,1\\}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nbe a latent state with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(Z=1)=0.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nConditional on\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>Z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nsuppose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>∼<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\mid Z=0\\sim\\mathcal N(-2,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∼<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\mid Z=1\\sim\\mathcal N(2,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe second parameter denotes the variance. Marginalising gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x)=0.7\\,\\varphi(x+2)+0.3\\,\\varphi(x-2),<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>where\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>φ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varphi<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis the standard normal density. The diagram shows the weighted\ncomponents and their sum. Sampling is straightforward: first draw the\nstate, then add standard normal noise. The laws of total expectation and\ntotal variance give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=-0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.7<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.3<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>4<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>4.36<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)=1+0.7\\cdot0.3\\cdot4^2=4.36<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDespite these moments, the mixture is not normally distributed.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">23<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>z<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rng.random(<span class=\"dv\">200000<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.3<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.where(z,<span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>rng.normal(size<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(z))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x.mean()<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.03<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(x.var()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">4.36<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.06<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(x.mean(),<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(x.var(),<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning and model comparison<\/h2>\n<p>When the density is accessible, maximum likelihood typically\nminimises the mean negative log-likelihood on training data. At\npopulation level, under suitable integrability conditions, this is\nequivalent to minimising\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{KL}(p_{\\mathrm{data}}\\|p_\\theta)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nup to a parameter-independent term. The direction matters: missing\nprobability mass where data occur is heavily penalised. Implicit models\nwithout an evaluable density need different criteria. Energy-based\nmodels\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∝<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(x)\\propto\\exp(-E_\\theta(x))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ncan likewise be difficult to train and sample from because their\nnormalising constant is unknown. MCMC from M18 is one possible\ncomponent.<\/p>\n<p>Separate fitting, hyperparameter selection and final evaluation. A\nmodel may reproduce training examples rather than capture the underlying\nstructure. Testing rare states and new operating conditions is therefore\nuseful alongside average performance.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Set the weight of the second state to 0.5. What are the resulting\nmoments? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[X]=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Var<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>16<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{Var}(X)=1+0.25\\cdot16=5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe density is symmetric; symmetry alone does not imply a normal\ndistribution. A generator sampling only from the first component loses\nan entire operating state.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://deepgenerativemodels.github.io/notes/introduction/\">Grover:\nDeep Generative Models – Introduction<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · teaching chapter opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M23-L2":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L2\">M23-L2 – Autoregressive Models and Likelihood<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Generative Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L1&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L1<\/a>;\nM18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Factorise a joint distribution\ninto conditional distributions and calculate log-likelihood and sampling\nprobabilities.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Chain rule and modelling<\/h2>\n<p>For a vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>…<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>d<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(x_1,\\ldots,x_d)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe chain rule gives<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><munderover><mo>∏<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>d<\/mi><\/munderover><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(x)=\\prod_{i=1}^d p_\\theta(x_i\\mid x_{&lt;i}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>This is initially an identity, not a conditional independence\nassumption. However, the variable order, context window and\nparameterisation affect learnability. In text, the variables are tokens;\nin measurement series, time points; and in images, for example, ordered\npixels or discrete image tokens. Every conditional distribution must be\nnormalised. Consequently, their product is also normalised, as can be\nseen by successively summing or integrating from the last entry back to\nthe first.<\/p>\n<p>During training, the actual preceding values are available. Causal\nmasking prevents a network from seeing the variable it must predict or\nany later values. During generation, only the prefix generated so far is\navailable; errors may therefore propagate.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Three binary measurement decisions<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001514_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three successive binary decisions show conditional probabilities and the marked path 1, 0, 1.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three successive binary decisions show\nconditional probabilities and the marked path 1, 0, 1.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Probability tree for a binary sequence.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>A sensor log contains three binary states. Suppose\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X_1=1)=0.6<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X_i=1\\mid X_{i-1}=1)=0.8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(X_i=1\\mid X_{i-1}=0)=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">i=2,3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis is a deliberately restricted first-order Markov model. The diagram\nshows its probability tree. The sequence\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(1,0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas probability\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.024<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6\\cdot0.2\\cdot0.2=0.024<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts negative log-likelihood is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.024<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>3.7297<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\log(0.024)\\approx3.7297<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nnats. In bits per variable, it is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.024<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.794<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\log_2(0.024)/3\\approx1.794<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> itertools <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> product<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> math <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> log,log2,isclose<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> b,q:q <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> b <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>q<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"kw\">def<\/span> prob(x):<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>    <span class=\"cf\">return<\/span> p(x[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p(x[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> x[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>] <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>p(x[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>],<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span> <span class=\"cf\">if<\/span> x[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>] <span class=\"cf\">else<\/span> <span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>{x:prob(x) <span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> x <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> product((<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>),repeat<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)}<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> isclose(<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(a.values()),<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> isclose(a[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],<span class=\"fl\">.024<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>log(a[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]),<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>log2(a[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Training and generation strategies<\/h2>\n<p>For a collection of sequences, the sum of the log conditional\nprobabilities is maximised. Given consistent tokenisation, mean negative\nlog-likelihood per token can be converted to perplexity\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><\/mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(\\mathrm{NLL}_{\\mathrm{Token}})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nComparing different tokenisations without further normalisation is\nproblematic. Variable sequence lengths require an explicit stopping\nrule, such as an end-of-sequence token; padding tokens are masked when\ncalculating the loss.<\/p>\n<p>Ancestral sampling draws successively from the conditional\ndistributions. Greedy decoding instead takes the maximum at every step;\nin general it is neither an unbiased sample nor the globally most\nprobable sequence. Temperature\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>T<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">T&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmodifies probabilities through\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">softmax<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>l<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{softmax}(l/T)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>l<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">l<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndenotes the logits. Top-k and top-p further restrict the candidate set.\nAll three interventions change the generated distribution. Computing\ntraining logits in parallel does not remove the sequential dependence of\nordinary sampling.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(1,1,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(0,0,0)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSolution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>0.8<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.384<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.6\\cdot0.8^2=0.384<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.4<\/mn><mo>⋅<\/mo><msup><mn>0.8<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.256<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.4\\cdot0.8^2=0.256<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nrespectively. Together, these two sequences are more probable than all\nsix sequences involving a transition. Three independent uniform random\nnumbers suffice to draw a sequence, each compared against the\nappropriate conditional probability.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://deepgenerativemodels.github.io/notes/autoregressive/\">Grover:\nDeep Generative Models – Autoregressive Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · teaching chapter\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M23-L3":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L3\">M23-L3 – Variational Autoencoders<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Generative Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L2&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L2<\/a>;\nM18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Derive a variational\nautoencoder’s ELBO and distinguish the reconstruction, KL term and\nreparameterisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Latent variables and the evidence bound<\/h2>\n<p>A VAE models data using a latent vector\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>z<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith prior\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand decoder\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(x\\mid z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe marginal likelihood\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mo>∫<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>d<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(x)=\\int p(z)p_\\theta(x\\mid z)\\,dz<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis often difficult to evaluate with nonlinear decoders. An encoder\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q_\\phi(z\\mid x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\napproximates the posterior. Substituting the joint model into the\ndefinition of KL divergence yields<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>θ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>ϕ<\/mi><mo>;<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p_\\theta(x)=\\mathcal L(\\theta,\\phi;x)+D_{KL}(q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\|p_\\theta(z\\mid x)),<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L=E_{q_\\phi}[\\log p_\\theta(x\\mid z)]-D_{KL}(q_\\phi(z\\mid x)\\|p(z)).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Because the final KL term in the first equation is non-negative,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi mathvariant=\"script\">ℒ<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mathcal L<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis a lower bound (ELBO). Maximising it improves the bound, but does not\nguarantee an exact posterior approximation when the encoder family is\nrestricted.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>A one-dimensional VAE teaching example<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001728_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Diagram of encoder and decoder, sampled latent variable, KL penalty and expected log-density term.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Diagram of encoder and decoder, sampled\nlatent variable, KL penalty and expected log-density term.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Variational autoencoder and two objective terms.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.25<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(z\\mid x)=\\mathcal N(1,0.25)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(z)=\\mathcal N(0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe KL term can be determined by integrating the two log normal\ndensities:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>μ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.81815<\/mn><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_{KL}=\\tfrac12(\\mu^2+\\sigma^2-1-\\log\\sigma^2)\\approx0.81815.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Let the decoder be\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>z<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p(x\\mid z)=\\mathcal N(2z,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the observation\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>z<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[(x-2z)^2]=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1.41894<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[\\log p(x\\mid z)]=-\\tfrac12(\\log(2\\pi)+1)\\approx-1.41894<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe ELBO is approximately\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.23709<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-2.23709<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere, the model even has an analytical marginal distribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X\\sim\\mathcal N(0,5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\ngiving\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>2.12366<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p(2)\\approx-2.12366<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthe gap is positive. The diagram arranges the encoder, latent sample,\ndecoder and both loss terms.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>mu,var<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.25<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>kl<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(mu<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>var<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.log(var))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rec<span class=\"op\">=-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(np.log(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>mu)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>var)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>true<span class=\"op\">=-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>(np.log(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi<span class=\"op\">*<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">4<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>elbo<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>rec<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>kl<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> elbo<span class=\"op\">&lt;=<\/span>true <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(kl,<span class=\"fl\">.81814718056<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(kl,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(elbo,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(true,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Differentiable sampling and limitations<\/h2>\n<p>For a diagonal Gaussian encoder, write\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>ϕ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>⊙<\/mo><mi>ϵ<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=\\mu_\\phi(x)+\\sigma_\\phi(x)\\odot\\epsilon<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis separates randomness from trainable parameters. The decoder loss\ncan then be differentiated along this computational path. For example, a\npositive standard deviation can be calculated from the predicted log\nvariance. This Gaussian trick does not apply directly to discrete latent\nvariables.<\/p>\n<p>The decoder distribution determines the reconstruction loss: a\nGaussian density with fixed variance gives squared errors up to\nconstants; a Bernoulli distribution gives binary cross-entropy.\nArbitrary weighting of the KL term (as in a\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>β<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>-VAE)\nchanges the objective. A very powerful decoder may ignore the latent\nvariables (posterior collapse). Unconditional generation samples new\nlatent values from the prior, not from the encoder for a particular\ntraining observation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Set\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>μ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>σ<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sigma^2=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThen\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the KL term is zero. In the decoder example above, however,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>z<\/mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[(2-2z)^2]=8<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nso the average reconstruction is considerably worse. A small KL term\nalone is therefore not a quality measure.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/1312.6114\">Kingma and Welling (2014):\nAuto-Encoding Variational Bayes<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article text opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M23-L4":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L4\">M23-L4 – Generative Adversarial Networks<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Generative Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L3&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L3<\/a>;\nM18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate the adversarial game,\ncalculate an optimal discriminator and recognise mode collapse.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two coupled learning objectives<\/h2>\n<p>A GAN transforms random input\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z\\sim p_z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ninto data using a generator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">G_\\theta(z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA discriminator\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>ψ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>∈<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_\\psi(x)\\in(0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndistinguishes generated samples from real data. The original minimax\ngame is<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/munder><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">max<\/mi><mi>ψ<\/mi><\/munder><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>ψ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>X<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>ψ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>G<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\min_\\theta\\max_\\psi V=E_{p_{\\mathrm{data}}}[\\log D_\\psi(X)]+E_{p_z}[\\log(1-D_\\psi(G_\\theta(Z)))].<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The parameter sets are updated alternately. During a discriminator\nstep, the generator is held fixed, and vice versa. Often the generator\ninstead minimises\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>z<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mi>D<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-E_z[\\log D(G(z))]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\n(the non-saturating variant). This provides a more favourable learning\nsignal when discriminator outputs are very small, but it is not the same\nobjective. The generator need not provide an evaluable density.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two modes as a discrete teaching example<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001579_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Grouped bars compare real, generator and discriminator probabilities for two generated categories.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Grouped bars compare real, generator and\ndiscriminator probabilities for two generated categories.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: GAN distribution mismatch.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Suppose the target distribution over two component shapes is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=(0.5,0.5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand the generator distribution is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.9<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=(0.9,0.1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith the generator fixed, an unconstrained discriminator can maximise\nthe contribution\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_i\\log D_i+q_i\\log(1-D_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin each state. Setting the derivative to zero and checking the negative\nsecond derivative gives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_i^*=p_i/(p_i+q_i)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwhen the total mass is positive. Thus\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>14<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.3571<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_1^*=5/14\\approx0.3571<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msubsup><mi>D<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo>*<\/mo><\/msubsup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>5<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>6<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.8333<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D_2^*=5/6\\approx0.8333<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows that the seldom-generated second shape is more\nfrequently judged real by the discriminator.<\/p>\n<p>Substituting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D^*<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>,<\/mo><mi>G<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><mo>+<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>J<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V(D^*,G)=-\\log4+2\\,JS(p,q)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nHere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>J<\/mi><mi>S<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\"><mfrac><mn>1<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/mfrac><\/mstyle><msub><mi>D<\/mi><mrow><mi>K<\/mi><mi>L<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>m<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">JS(p,q)=\\tfrac12 D_{KL}(p\\|m)+\\tfrac12 D_{KL}(q\\|m)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwhere\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>m<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">m=(p+q)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nis the Jensen–Shannon divergence. At the idealised optimum\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D^*=1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\non the common support. This says nothing about convergence of finite\nneural-network training.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>p<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([<span class=\"fl\">.9<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>])<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> d<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>p<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(p<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>q)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> m<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(p<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>q)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>js<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(p<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.log(p<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>m)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>q<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.log(q<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>m))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(p<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.log(d)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>q<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.log1p(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>d))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(d,[<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">14<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span><span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">6<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(v,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.log(<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>js)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(d,<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(js,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(v,<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Stability and variants<\/h2>\n<p>A generator that almost always produces the same convincing shape may\nhave little diversity. With mode collapse, important regions of the\ntarget distribution are missing. An overly strong discriminator, poorly\ncoordinated learning rates and non-stationary interactions between\nnetworks can make optimisation difficult. Monitoring only one loss value\nis insufficient. Use fixed latent test samples, independent real\ncomparison data and coverage metrics.<\/p>\n<p>Wasserstein GANs replace binary classification with a real-valued\ncritic subject to a Lipschitz constraint. The corresponding distance is\nexplored in <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L8&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L8<\/a>.\nPractical approximations of Lipschitz constraints, such as gradient\npenalties, are not exact global guarantees.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q=(0.5,0.5)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msup><mi>D<\/mi><mo>*<\/mo><\/msup><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">D^*=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin both states and\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>V<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>4<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">V=-\\log 4<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIf instead a discriminator outputs a constant 0.5 even when\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≠<\/mo><mi>q<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\ne q<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nits loss alone is misleading: a poorly trained discriminator is not\nevidence that the generator distribution matches the target.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2014/hash/f033ed80deb0234979a61f95710dbe25-Abstract.html\">Goodfellow\net al. (2014): Generative Adversarial Nets<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://deepgenerativemodels.github.io/notes/gan/\">Grover:\nDeep Generative Models – Generative Adversarial Networks<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · teaching\nchapter opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M23-L5":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L5\">M23-L5 – Normalising Flows<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Generative Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L4&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L4<\/a>;\nM18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Apply the change-of-variables\nformula, analyse an invertible flow and understand the role of the\nJacobian determinant.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Invertible transformations<\/h2>\n<p>A normalising flow defines\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>f<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>Z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=f_\\theta(Z)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor an easily evaluated base density\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>Z<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_Z<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand a bijective, differentiable mapping with a differentiable inverse.\nThe dimension remains unchanged. Probability mass is preserved, but\nlocal volume changes. The density must therefore be corrected by the\ndeterminant of the inverse Jacobian:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>Z<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>J<\/mi><msup><mi>f<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log p_X(x)=\\log p_Z(f^{-1}(x))+\\log|\\det J_{f^{-1}}(x)|.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>Alternatively, the second term can be written as\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>z<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\log|\\det J_f(z)|<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor a composition of transformations, the log determinants add along the\nsequence of intermediate states. To sample, transformations are applied\nforward; to evaluate density, they are applied in reverse.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Affine transformation as a teaching example<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001505_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Standard normal density compared with a normal density with mean 3 and variance 4.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Standard normal density compared with a\nnormal density with mean 3 and variance 4.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Affine transformation of a probability density.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>Let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">Z\\sim\\mathcal N(0,1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>X<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mi>Z<\/mi><mo>+<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X=2Z+3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe inverse mapping is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(x-3)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwith derivative\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe density is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>X<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>φ<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_X(x)=\\varphi((x-3)/2)/2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe mean becomes 3 and the variance 4. At the peak\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe density is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><mi>π<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.19947<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/(2\\sqrt{2\\pi})\\approx0.19947<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram shows the broadened and shifted density. If the factor 1/2\nis omitted, the curve integrates to 2 rather than 1.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.integrate <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> quad<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>phi<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> z:np.exp(<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>z<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>z<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>np.pi)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>f<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> z:<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">*<\/span>z<span class=\"op\">+<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>inv<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> x:(x<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>px<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"kw\">lambda<\/span> x:phi(inv(x))<span class=\"op\">/<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>v<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>quad(px,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.inf,np.inf)[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>]<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(v,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(inv(f(np.array([<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]))),[<span class=\"op\">-<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(px(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>),<span class=\"dv\">5<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(v,<span class=\"dv\">8<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Efficient coupling layers<\/h2>\n<p>An affine coupling layer splits a vector into\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z_a,z_b<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand sets\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_a=z_a<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>b<\/mi><\/msub><mo>⊙<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_b=z_b\\odot\\exp(s(z_a))+t(z_a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe first subset remains unchanged and supplies the parameters for the\nrest. To invert, subtract\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t(x_a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand multiply by\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\exp(-s(x_a))<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe Jacobian is block triangular, so\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">det<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">|<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>s<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mi>a<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\log|\\det J_f|=\\sum_j s_j(z_a)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAlternating partitions and permutations can affect every component.\nExtremely large scaling factors can cause numerical problems.<\/p>\n<p>Flow models permit exact likelihood evaluation within the specified\nmodel when inversion and determinants can be evaluated exactly. They do\nnot guarantee good semantic modelling. A continuous density for discrete\npixels also needs an appropriate discretisation or dequantisation\nmethod. The class of ordinary bijective flows has topological\nlimitations: for example, it cannot map a connected support to two\nexactly disconnected supports.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_1=z_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>3<\/mn><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>z<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_2=3z_2+z_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe Jacobian is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>J<\/mi><mi>f<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>3<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">J_f=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;0\\\\1&amp;3\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nIts determinant is 3, and the log correction when evaluating density is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">-\\log3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFor\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>x<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>8<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x=(2,8)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe obtain\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>z<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">z=(2,2)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSubstitution into the forward mapping returns the original point.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://www.jmlr.org/papers/v22/19-1028.html\">Papamakarios\net al. (2021): Normalizing Flows for Probabilistic Modeling and\nInference<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://deepgenerativemodels.github.io/notes/flow/\">Grover:\nDeep Generative Models – Normalizing Flow Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · teaching chapter\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M23-L6":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L6\">M23-L6 – Diffusion Models and Score Matching<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Generative Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L5&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L5<\/a>;\nM18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Understand a DDPM forward\nprocess, its noise-prediction objective and the score through an\nanalytical example.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Gradual addition of noise<\/h2>\n<p>A discrete diffusion model defines a Markov chain that gradually\nmixes data\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith Gaussian noise. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>&lt;<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>&lt;<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0&lt;\\beta_t&lt;1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\alpha_t=1-\\beta_t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mo>∏<\/mo><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><mi>t<\/mi><\/msubsup><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>s<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar\\alpha_t=\\prod_{s=1}^t\\alpha_s<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nwe have<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msqrt><msub><mi>α<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mrow><mi>t<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>,<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">q(x_t\\mid x_{t-1})=\\mathcal N(\\sqrt{\\alpha_t}x_{t-1},\\beta_t I),<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"2.0em\"><\/mspace><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mo>∼<\/mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">𝒩<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mi>I<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_t=\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_t}x_0+\\sqrt{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}\\epsilon,\\qquad \\epsilon\\sim\\mathcal N(0,I).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The second equation follows by combining independent, normally\ndistributed noise contributions: their variances add. When\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>T<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar\\alpha_T<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nis sufficiently small, the final distribution approximates a standard\nnormal distribution under suitable assumptions about the data. The\nreverse direction is approximated by trainable transitions. The system\ndoes not preserve an individual original data point; instead, it models\na distribution of possible data.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Signal, noise component and score<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001552_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Three conditional Gaussian densities become wider and their means move during the forward diffusion process.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Three conditional Gaussian densities\nbecome wider and their means move during the forward diffusion\nprocess.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Forward diffusion for a fixed observation.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>For three teaching steps with constant\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>β<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\beta=0.2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.512<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar\\alpha_3=0.512<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0=2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>X<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">X_3\\mid x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas mean\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>2<\/mn><msqrt><mn>0.512<\/mn><\/msqrt><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.4311<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">2\\sqrt{0.512}\\approx1.4311<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand variance 0.488. Taking\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon=-1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ngives\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>3<\/mn><\/msub><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>0.7325<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_3\\approx0.7325<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe score of a density is its spatial log gradient; for the conditional\ndensity,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>s<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>∣<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msqrt><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msqrt><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">s(x_t\\mid x_0)=-(x_t-\\sqrt{\\bar\\alpha_t}x_0)/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)=-\\epsilon/\\sqrt{1-\\bar\\alpha_t}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nAt this sample point, it is approximately 1.4315. The diagram shows\nconditional densities for the three steps; this short chain does not yet\ngive a useful terminal prior.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>b<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> a<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">3<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> x0<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"fl\">2.<\/span><span class=\"op\">;<\/span> e<span class=\"op\">=-<\/span><span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>x<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.sqrt(a)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x0<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>e<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>s<span class=\"op\">=-<\/span>(x<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.sqrt(a)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x0)<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(s,<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>e<span class=\"op\">/<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>rng<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.random.default_rng(<span class=\"dv\">23<\/span>)<span class=\"op\">;<\/span> u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.full(<span class=\"dv\">150000<\/span>,x0)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">for<\/span> _ <span class=\"kw\">in<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">range<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>): u<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.sqrt(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>b)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>u<span class=\"op\">+<\/span>np.sqrt(b)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>rng.normal(size<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"bu\">len<\/span>(u))<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(u.mean()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>np.sqrt(a)<span class=\"op\">*<\/span>x0)<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.015<\/span> <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> <span class=\"bu\">abs<\/span>(u.var()<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><span class=\"op\">-<\/span>a))<span class=\"op\">&lt;<\/span><span class=\"fl\">.015<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(x,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(s,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>),<span class=\"bu\">round<\/span>(u.var(),<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>))<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Learning noise and generating in reverse<\/h2>\n<p>A common DDPM training objective selects a time\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>t<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfrom the training steps, creates artificial noise and trains a network\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\epsilon_\\theta(x_t,t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith mean squared error\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>E<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">E[\\|\\epsilon-\\epsilon_\\theta(x_t,t)\\|^2]<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe optimal predictor under squared loss is the conditional expectation\nof the noise given\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_t,t<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis yields an estimator of the marginal score\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>q<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_{x_t}\\log q_t(x_t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nnot the score conditioned on an individual known\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mn>0<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">x_0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDistinguish forward training noise from reverse-time sampling.<\/p>\n<p>A common parameterisation of the reverse mean is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msubsup><mi>α<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><mrow><mi>−<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>β<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>ϵ<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><mi>t<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>/<\/mi><msqrt><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><\/msqrt><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\mu_\\theta(x_t,t)=\\alpha_t^{-1/2}(x_t-\\beta_t\\epsilon_\\theta(x_t,t)/\\sqrt{1-\\bar\\alpha_t})<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe reverse variance must be set or modelled separately. Generation can\nstart with normally distributed noise and repeatedly apply reverse\nsteps. Score-based SDE models express the same underlying idea in\ncontinuous time. Numerical methods and the number of steps affect both\nruntime and quality. Latent diffusion works in a learned representation\nspace; its decoder introduces another possible source of error.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Calculate the signal-to-noise variance ratio\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mover><mi>α<\/mi><mo accent=\"true\">‾<\/mo><\/mover><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\bar\\alpha_t/(1-\\bar\\alpha_t)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor the three steps, assuming standardised data variance. Solution: 4,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.64<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.36<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.778<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.64/0.36\\approx1.778<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.512<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.488<\/mn><mo>≈<\/mo><mn>1.049<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.512/0.488\\approx1.049<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe relative signal contribution decreases. It must decrease further to\nobtain an approximately normal terminal prior.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2020/hash/4c5bcfec8584af0d967f1ab10179ca4b-Abstract.html\">Ho\net al. (2020): Denoising Diffusion Probabilistic Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract\nopened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.13456\">Song et al. (2021):\nScore-Based Generative Modeling through Stochastic Differential\nEquations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article text opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M23-L7":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L7\">M23-L7 – Evaluation, Conditioning and Limitations of Generative Models<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Generative Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Compulsory elective/models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L6&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L6<\/a>;\nM18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Assess conditional generation,\ndensity and sample metrics, quality limitations and privacy risks\nseparately.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Conditioning and guidance<\/h2>\n<p>A condition\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>c<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">c<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nmay be a class, a text, a measurement or a partially observed vector. A\nconditional generative model should represent\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>θ<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p_\\theta(x\\mid c)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nDuring training, data and conditions must be correctly paired. A model\nthat ignores conditions may produce globally plausible samples and\nnevertheless fail the task. Classifier-based guidance in diffusion uses\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi>∇<\/mi><mi>x<\/mi><\/msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>p<\/mi><mi>t<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>c<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mi>x<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\nabla_x\\log p_t(x\\mid c)=\\nabla_x\\log p_t(x)+\\nabla_x\\log p_t(c\\mid x)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>;\nthis identity holds for consistent densities and positive probabilities.\nClassifier-free guidance combines conditional and unconditional network\npredictions without requiring a separately trained classifier.\nExtrapolating guidance factors alter the generated distribution and can\nreduce diversity.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Two models, different quality characteristics<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001581_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Generator G1 scores higher on a simplified precision measure while G2 has greater coverage.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Generator G1 scores higher on a\nsimplified precision measure while G2 has greater coverage.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Generative precision and coverage.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>In a synthetic teaching example, real data comprise two allowed\ncategories, A and B, both occurring with positive frequency. Generator\nG1 produces only A; G2 produces A and B as well as an invalid category\nC, with probabilities 0.4, 0.4 and 0.2. Here, deliberately simplified\nprecision is the generated probability mass on the allowed support;\nsimplified coverage is the proportion of real categories produced at\nall. G1 achieves 1 and 0.5, whereas G2 achieves 0.8 and 1. The diagram\nshows that the two aspects of quality favour different model choices.\nThis is not an implementation of image-based precision–recall using\nneighbourhood sets.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>q<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"fl\">1.<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.2<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>precision<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>q[:,:<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>].<span class=\"bu\">sum<\/span>(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>coverage<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(q[:,:<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>]<span class=\"op\">&gt;<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>).mean(axis<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.allclose(precision,[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.8<\/span>]) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(coverage,[<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"co\"># One-dimensional Gaussian feature distance, analogous to FID<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>m1,m2,s1,s2<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>fd<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(m1<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>m2)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><span class=\"op\">+<\/span>(s1<span class=\"op\">-<\/span>s2)<span class=\"op\">**<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> fd<span class=\"op\">==<\/span><span class=\"dv\">2<\/span><\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(precision,coverage,fd)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Comparable and reproducible evaluation<\/h2>\n<p>FID compares means and covariances of features from real and\ngenerated images under a Gaussian approximation:<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mn>2<\/mn><\/msubsup><mo>+<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">tr<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>+<\/mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msubsup><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><msubsup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">Σ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msubsup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>2<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\|\\mu_r-\\mu_g\\|_2^2+\\operatorname{tr}(\\Sigma_r+\\Sigma_g-2(\\Sigma_r^{1/2}\\Sigma_g\\Sigma_r^{1/2})^{1/2}).<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>In one dimension, this simplifies to\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>μ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><mo>+<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>r<\/mi><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>σ<\/mi><mi>g<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mn>2<\/mn><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">(\\mu_r-\\mu_g)^2+(\\sigma_r-\\sigma_g)^2<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWith means of 0 and 1 and standard deviations of 2 and 1, the result is\n2. A lower FID indicates a better match only in this feature-and-moment\nrepresentation, not necessarily images that look better to humans. The\nfeature extractor, preprocessing and sample size must be held constant\nin comparisons. Log-likelihood is a different quantity and is not\ndirectly available for every model.<\/p>\n<p>An evaluation report should give samples, coverage, error classes,\nrepetitions and uncertainty for each condition. Separate similarity and\nprivacy tests are needed to detect memorised training data; synthetic\ndata are not automatically anonymous. Text and code require technically\nverified content and functional tests. Plausible-sounding prose does not\nestablish truth. Mark artificially generated test cases and check\nwhether they are permissible for later use.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>For G2, discard C after generation without changing anything else.\nWhat distribution results among accepted samples? Solution:\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>P<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>A<\/mi><mo>∣<\/mo><mtext mathvariant=\"normal\">accepted<\/mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.4<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mn>0.8<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">P(A\\mid\\text{accepted})=0.4/0.8=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nand likewise for B. The acceptance rate is 0.8. Simplified precision\nincreases to 1; with other weights or rare categories, however,\nfiltering can introduce new biases.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://arxiv.org/abs/2011.13456\">Song et al. (2021):\nScore-Based Generative Modeling through Stochastic Differential\nEquations<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · article text opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper_files/paper/2017/hash/8a1d694707eb0fefe65871369074926d-Abstract.html\">Heusel\net al. (2017): GANs Trained by a Two Time-Scale Update Rule Converge to\na Local Nash Equilibrium<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract opened<\/div><\/li>\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a\nhref=\"https://proceedings.neurips.cc/paper/2019/hash/0234c510bc6d908b28c70ff313743079-Abstract.html\">Kynkäänniemi\net al. (2019): Improved Precision and Recall Metric for Assessing\nGenerative Models<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · abstract opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>","university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence_en:M23-L8":"<article class=\"lesson-standard\"><h1 id=\"L8\">M23-L8 – Optimal Transport and Distances between Distributions<\/h1>\n<p><strong>Course area:<\/strong> M23 – Generative Models<\/p>\n<p><strong>Level:<\/strong> Master · Advanced elective<\/p>\n<p><strong>Prerequisites:<\/strong> <a\nhref=\"index.html?course=university_data-science-and-artificial-intelligence&amp;module=M23&amp;lesson=L7&amp;syslang=en&amp;doclang=en&amp;theme=light\">M23-L7<\/a>;\nM18, M19.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Learning objective:<\/strong> Formulate a discrete transport\nproblem, interpret the Wasserstein distance geometrically and explain\nregularisation.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S1\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>From probability mass to transport plans<\/h2>\n<p>KL divergences compare probabilities at the same location. Optimal\ntransport additionally accounts for the geometry of the space: how much\nmass must move, and how far, to transform one distribution into another?\nFor discrete distributions with weights\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">a_i,b_j\\ge0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\neach summing to 1, let\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>d<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>x<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><mo>,<\/mo><msub><mi>y<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>p<\/mi><\/msup><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C_{ij}=d(x_i,y_j)^p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nfor a metric\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>d<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">d<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p\\ge1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nA transport plan\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{ij}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nhas marginals\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>a<\/mi><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_j\\pi_{ij}=a_i<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mi>i<\/mi><\/msub><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>b<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\sum_i\\pi_{ij}=b_j<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand non-negative entries. Then<\/p>\n<p><math display=\"block\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msup><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><munder><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">min<\/mi><mrow><mi>π<\/mi><mo>≥<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><mi>π<\/mi><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>a<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\"><\/mspace><msup><mi>π<\/mi><mi>T<\/mi><\/msup><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">𝟏<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mi>b<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><munder><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/munder><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mn>1<\/mn><mi>/<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msup><mi>.<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_p(a,b)=\\left(\\min_{\\pi\\ge0,\\,\\pi\\mathbf1=a,\\,\\pi^T\\mathbf1=b}\\sum_{ij}C_{ij}\\pi_{ij}\\right)^{1/p}.<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math><\/p>\n<p>The discrete problem is a linear optimisation problem in the plan\nvariables. A coupling can split mass, unlike a deterministic transport\nmapping. On general spaces,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mi>p<\/mi><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nrequires finite\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mi>p<\/mi><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>th\nmoments.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S2\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Transport along a measurement axis<\/h2>\n<figure>\n<img src=\"../learning-courses-university-data-science-and-artificial-intelligence-en/media/img/001553_en.svg\"\nalt=\"Source masses at 0 and 3 are transported to targets at 1 and 4 via three weighted flows.\" />\n<figcaption aria-hidden=\"true\">Source masses at 0 and 3 are transported\nto targets at 1 and 4 via three weighted flows.<\/figcaption>\n<\/figure>\n<p><em>Figure: Discrete optimal transport along a line.<\/em><\/p>\n<p>The source distribution places mass 0.6 at location 0 and 0.4 at\nlocation 3. The target places 0.5 at each of locations 1 and 4. For\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>p<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">p=1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand absolute distance, the cost matrix is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>C<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>2<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">C=\\begin{pmatrix}1&amp;4\\\\2&amp;1\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nWriting\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mn>11<\/mn><\/msub><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=\\pi_{11}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nthe marginal constraints give\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mn>12<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.6<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{12}=0.6-u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mn>21<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{21}=0.5-u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mn>22<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>−<\/mo><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\pi_{22}=u-0.1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nFeasibility requires\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0.1<\/mn><mo>≤<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0.1\\le u\\le0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe cost is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>3.3<\/mn><mo>−<\/mo><mn>4<\/mn><mi>u<\/mi><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">3.3-4u<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>,\nminimised at\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>u<\/mi><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">u=0.5<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe optimal plan is\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mtable><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0.5<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0.1<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><mtr><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0<\/mn><\/mtd><mtd columnalign=\"center\" class=\"text-center\"><mn>0.4<\/mn><\/mtd><\/mtr><\/mtable><mo stretchy=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\begin{pmatrix}0.5&amp;0.1\\\\0&amp;0.4\\end{pmatrix}<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nand\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mn>1.3<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_1=1.3<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThe diagram displays the mass flow between four locations; line widths\nrepresent transported mass.<\/p>\n<div class=\"sourceCode\" id=\"cb1\"><pre\nclass=\"sourceCode python\"><code class=\"sourceCode python\"><span id=\"cb1-1\"><a href=\"#cb1-1\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">import<\/span> numpy <span class=\"im\">as<\/span> np<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-2\"><a href=\"#cb1-2\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.optimize <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> linprog<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-3\"><a href=\"#cb1-3\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"im\">from<\/span> scipy.stats <span class=\"im\">import<\/span> wasserstein_distance<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-4\"><a href=\"#cb1-4\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>C<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-5\"><a href=\"#cb1-5\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>A<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>np.array([[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-6\"><a href=\"#cb1-6\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>r<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>linprog(C.ravel(),A_eq<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>A,b_eq<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>[<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>],bounds<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>(<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"va\">None<\/span>),method<span class=\"op\">=<\/span><span class=\"st\">&#39;highs&#39;<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-7\"><a href=\"#cb1-7\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> r.success <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.allclose(r.x.reshape(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),[[<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.1<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>]])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-8\"><a href=\"#cb1-8\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a>w<span class=\"op\">=<\/span>wasserstein_distance([<span class=\"dv\">0<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">3<\/span>],[<span class=\"dv\">1<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">4<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">.6<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.4<\/span>],[<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>,<span class=\"fl\">.5<\/span>])<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-9\"><a href=\"#cb1-9\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"cf\">assert<\/span> np.isclose(r.fun,w) <span class=\"kw\">and<\/span> np.isclose(w,<span class=\"fl\">1.3<\/span>)<\/span>\n<span id=\"cb1-10\"><a href=\"#cb1-10\" aria-hidden=\"true\" tabindex=\"-1\"><\/a><span class=\"bu\">print<\/span>(r.x.reshape(<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>,<span class=\"dv\">2<\/span>),w)<\/span><\/code><\/pre><\/div>\n<h2 id=\"S3\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Entropic regularisation and links to modelling<\/h2>\n<p>Large cost matrices are computationally expensive. One common\napproximation minimises\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">⟨<\/mo><mi>C<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>π<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">⟩<\/mo><mo>+<\/mo><mi>ε<\/mi><msub><mo>∑<\/mo><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><msub><mi>π<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>−<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\langle C,\\pi\\rangle+\\varepsilon\\sum_{ij}\\pi_{ij}(\\log\\pi_{ij}-1)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nunder the same marginal constraints, with\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mi>ε<\/mi><mo>&gt;<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\varepsilon&gt;0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nin units of cost and the convention\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">log<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mn>0<\/mn><mo>=<\/mo><mn>0<\/mn><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">0\\log0=0<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nUnder suitable positive weights, the solution is a scaled matrix\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>u<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">diag<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>v<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">\\operatorname{diag}(u)K\\operatorname{diag}(v)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nwith\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>K<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>=<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">exp<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>−<\/mi><msub><mi>C<\/mi><mrow><mi>i<\/mi><mi>j<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mi>/<\/mi><mi>ε<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">K_{ij}=\\exp(-C_{ij}/\\varepsilon)<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nSinkhorn iterations alternately adjust the scaling vectors to fit the\ntwo marginals. Small regularisation can cause numerical underflow;\nlog-domain implementations help. Regularised transport costs are not\nexactly the Wasserstein distance, and self-costs in particular can be\nnon-zero.<\/p>\n<p>Under suitable assumptions,\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\nobeys Kantorovich–Rubinstein duality\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><mi>P<\/mi><mo>,<\/mo><mi>Q<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><mo>=<\/mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">sup<\/mi><mo>&#8289;<\/mo><\/mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">∥<\/mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i<\/mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p<\/mi><\/mrow><\/msub><mo>≤<\/mo><mn>1<\/mn><\/mrow><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">(<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>P<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo>−<\/mo><msub><mi>E<\/mi><mi>Q<\/mi><\/msub><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"prefix\">[<\/mo><mi>f<\/mi><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">]<\/mo><mo stretchy=\"false\" form=\"postfix\">)<\/mo><\/mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_1(P,Q)=\\sup_{\\|f\\|_{\\mathrm{Lip}}\\le1}(E_P[f]-E_Q[f])<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>.\nThis motivates a Lipschitz critic in generative models. Unlike KL\ndivergence, the distance between two point masses at different locations\nremains finite and accounts for their separation. Its meaning\nnevertheless depends on the chosen metric or feature representation;\nestimating it from high-dimensional samples can be difficult.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S4\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Exercise with solution<\/h2>\n<p>Compare point masses at 0 and 2 with point masses at 0 and 10. Their\nrespective\n<math display=\"inline\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><msub><mi>W<\/mi><mn>1<\/mn><\/msub><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">W_1<\/annotation><\/semantics><\/math>\ndistances are 2 and 10, as the entire unit mass must be moved. In both\ncases the KL divergence in the direction of the first point mass is\ninfinite, because the second distribution assigns no mass at 0. The\nWasserstein distance therefore distinguishes the two cases\ngeometrically.<\/p>\n<h2 id=\"S5\"><span class=\"section-number\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><\/span>Sources and further reading<\/h2>\n<ul class=\"lc-content-list\" role=\"list\">\n<li role=\"listitem\"><span class=\"lc-item-marker\" aria-hidden=\"true\">•<\/span><div class=\"lc-item-content\"><a href=\"https://optimaltransport.github.io/book/\">Peyré and Cuturi\n(2019): Computational Optimal Transport<span class=\"lc-external-arrow\" aria-hidden=\"true\">&#8239;↗<\/span><span class=\"sr-only lc-external-hint\"> (external link)<\/span><\/a> · book overview opened<\/div><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/article>"};
